ons that  i had about technical interviews like  what are they exactly and what are they  meant to do and how did we even get here  are technical interviews even like the  best way to evaluate someone's skills in  today's world or is it actually kind of  a gatekeeping mechanism for those  entering the industry right now are  there any other alternatives out there  well this month all of those questions  kind of started to boil over so i  decided to finally tackle this topic i  did my research through articles and  papers and interviews some in part  thanks to the sponsor of today's video  formation which trains their fellows to  land jobs at top tech companies i was  able to meet and talk to members of  their team who are really knowledgeable  in this topic to learn more and i'll  tell you more about formation later in  the video so stick around  so i graduated from college in 2014 so a  lot of my technical interview experience  and knowledge was from around that time  when i was looking for my very first job  as a software engineer but of course the  whole field and industry had been around  for a while at that point which meant  that technical interviews were pretty  common then too but you know i never  thought to ask the question until now  how do we get here like where do  technical interviews even come from well  before we had our current industry we  had the dot com era and before that with  semiconductors and before that  well you get the idea so during the  dot-com era when software engineers had  their first meteoric rise to hype  popularity and power  microsoft was arguably the most dominant  player in tech at the time during that  time the tide was changing from these  old well-established companies owning  the industry to a fast-paced uncertain  high-risk but high-reward tech scene and  for microsoft at the time windows was  the most widely used gui operating  system in the world and microsoft office  was taking the world by store so they  needed to figure out a way to hire the  best people in the market the 2003 book  by william poundstone describes how  microsoft hired people in that they  needed to find the most intelligent  resourceful and out-of-the-box thinkers  needed to survive in the  hyper-competitive business world bill  gates the founder was a huge fan of  puzzles and riddles and that became one  of the main parts of the interview  process these are the times by the way  when they would ask questions like how  would you move mount fuji or how many  piano tuners are there out in the world  there are other parts of the interview  of course like technical overviews of  past projects you've worked on or  abstract design questions but by far the  puzzles and riddles questions had the  most buzz they believed that through  asking those questions they could see if  someone would be willing to challenge  assumptions and see things from novel  perspectives which in essence is asking  someone to demonstrate their  problem-solving abilities of course you  don't hear much about those like puzzle  and riddle type questions being asked in  the modern day interview because the  industry's morphed and evolved since the  dot-com era but you see the intentions  of those questions being reflected in  the modern day technical interview and  the need to test someone's abilities in  solving novel problems  now before we go further we need to  define what we mean by technical  interview because it's kind of a loaded  term and that's because there's a lot of  different kinds of technical interviews  out there the most common by far is the  data structures and algorithms interview  they're typically conducted in a 45 to  60 minute session in which an  interviewer guides a candidate through a  coding exercise so in these data  structures and algorithms questions you  might get questions like how do you  check if two strings are anagrams of  each other how is a binary search tree  implemented how is a bubble sort  algorithm implemented other than these  data structures and algorithms  interviews there's a lot of different  kinds of technical interviews too  there's system design interviews live  coding interviews pair programming  interviews take-home projects and  technical behavioral interviews always  test different skills for software  engineers at different companies for  different skills and at differing levels  all these interviews though are roughly  trying to test for a similar thing  you're looking for constructive problem  solving technique you're looking for  fluent coding and effective coding and  in general i would call i would say  that's coding in a way that clearly  communicates the idea or translates the  idea  from the problem-solving phase into code  in a way  that avoids most of the bugs we all  write bugs right so like the expectation  is not that there are zero bugs at the  outset  but it's that you write your code in a  way that shows that you know how to  avoid common mistakes and then of course  at the end you want the code to be  bug-free so there's a there's a testing  component like can you now explore the  code you've written effectively write  the tests fix any bugs that are there  that's daniel tomko a software engineer  who helped craft a lot of facebook's  interview process but by far the most  common technical interview is the data  structures and algorithms interview so  i'm gonna primarily focus on that one  for this video meet adam he's a fourth  year computer science student at the  university of waterloo and he is no  stranger to technical interviews i've  done technical interviews at like dozens  of companies i guess like notable ones  would be like facebook and citadel  shopify square sigma so like mostly on  like the tech company some fintech  company kind of range i decided to  interview adam to understand what the  technical interview process is like for  students who are entering the industry  right now because you know the tech  industry changes at like a rapid speed  and i wanted to get a pulse i just felt  that it was just luck basically that if  you had seen the question on leak code  before  then you would be able to solve it i  knew like to a certain extent that you  could learn a lot of these concepts and  be able to do them but it felt like it  was such a grind to get to that point  that and it was so unrelated to the job  that you would be doing it wasn't a  great metric of my actual skills like i  was confident in my abilities as a  programmer  especially like building apps and stuff  but i wasn't confident in my leak code  abilities and i thought how can that be  like how can it be that i wouldn't do  well in the interview but i think i  would do well on the job i also think  that if you get a bad interviewer it can  feel pretty disheartening if you go in  and you you're supposed to like this  novel question  that doesn't seem like there's any way  you could have figured it out and the  interviewer isn't trying to push you  along and give you pointers and tips  then like you're gonna walk out of that  feeling like you got railroaded and i've  had that happen a couple times where  like afterwards i'm like i don't think  there's anything i could have done  differently now in a nutshell from what  i can understand from adam and hearing  from others who are breaking into tech  these are the reasons why technical  interviews feel so broken they're not a  great way of assessing technical ability  because what they test you on is so  different from what you're actually  going to be doing on the job and that  sometimes if you don't get a great  interviewer or an interview question it  can be really disheartening and cost you  the job i also asked people who were  really well versed with technical  interviews to get an opinion on the  other side and you know what  they said the same thing about them i  think the biggest con it's super obvious  is that it's not similar to day-to-day  work it's not simila