ally there's several  categories that a candidate is assessed  on using some sort of scale which  ultimately ladders up to whether that  interviewer thinks that the candidate  would be good for the job i think what's  broken about coding interviews  is if you don't have a rubric  that's deeply connected  to what's required for success on the  job then you're gonna end up with a  bunch of different problems number one  you're not gonna get the right people  number two you may end up with a  diversity problem because one one way  like if your rubric is do they solve the  problem your your temptation might  actually be to just ask really hard  questions and if you're asking really  hard questions then you're you're  avoiding false positives right well they  solve this really hard problem so they  must be good right but what then what  you're doing and that might be true but  what you're doing is then you're  selecting for people that either  spent the last year on lead code or have  a cs degree from a top-notch university  and so you're you're eliminating people  that might actually have a lot of  ability and might be great engineers  but maybe don't have the same the same  background instead if you have a really  clear rubric across a bunch of different  signal areas and then you're evaluating  against that very carefully then you can  kind of unbreak the interview to some  degree so let's walk through one of  these rubrics to show you what they look  like medium posted their grading rubric  for engineering interviews in 2016. it's  probably outdated at this point since  it's six years old but i still think  it's a good example so let's go over it  the categories that they graded on in  the technical interview are the ability  to build software the ability to learn  and teach and a values alignment in each  of these categories they define the  signals they're looking for during the  interview for example in the category  for the ability to build software  they're looking for someone who can  demonstrate problem solving abilities  code fluency autonomy has a basic  understanding of computer science  knowledge demonstrates resoluteness in  finding a solution and for senior  positions has strong skills in systems  design  they grade each on a five point scale  strong no  no  mixed yes and strong yes and give  examples of what each look like in each  of these categories  so for example in problem solving a  strong no is when the candidate is  unable to conceive of any solution to  the problem  the candidate is able to describe a  basic or naive approach but is unable to  implement it and a strong yes is when  the candidate clarifies the scope as a  natural first step is able to articulate  multiple approaches and describe the  pros and cons of each makes a sensible  decision based on the constraints of the  interview format and is able to fully  implement the approach without any real  help from the interviewer the candidate  recognizes edge cases and draws  attention to them unprompted then solves  them these rubrics are really helpful in  calibrating interviewers on what a great  interview looks like without a rubric  interviewers might grade candidates on  the wrong thing like things that are not  that important to a company things like  getting the right or wrong answer or  getting to that answer in record speed  accompanies where they have these  rubrics it's usually a core group of  really passionate people who want to  improve the interview experience who  make it but again in order to make one  that fully aligns with the team's goals  and values it really does take time  energy and effort not to mention once  this rubric is made there's a larger  group of interviewers that need to be  trained to interview in a way that  aligns with a rubric it takes a village  to evaluate someone's skills so everyone  gets involved somehow whether that be  hosting technical interviews or  behavioral interviews or doing phone  screens or gett