oes things differently so if this  question was thrown at you during an  interview it'd kind of be a miracle if  you can get it done because being an  engineer is not just about coding i  actually really love how sophie put it  being an engineer is really about  combining programming logic with  business logic you are trying to build a  sign up experience where when you fill  out this information and you click this  button an account gets created and to do  that you're going to write some code to  make that logic happen and so in order  to give candidates a fair chance at  solving a problem within a scoped period  of time you're gonna need to give them a  smaller example so what kinds of  questions can companies give to  candidates granted they need to make  questions that work well no matter who  the candidate is because there's a lot  of variables whether the candidate  started to learn how to code in python  java or javascript where they learned  how to code what tech stacks they worked  in previously what product and services  they worked on before the processes  they've used for building and deploying  code  among many many others as a company how  would you find a question that is  agnostic of all of that so you can find  the right person for the job well it  goes back to the fundamental building  blocks of software engineering data  structures and algorithms what data  structures and algorithms is doing is  it's really removing  all of the fluff i would say of the  business logic layer and really hyper  focusing on the underlying programmatic  logic only so that's why a lot of data  structures you're working with nodes so  like you're working with a linked list  or a binary tree and each node has no  meaning in like your day-to-day  programming that node might be a profile  object that is connected to another  piece of data that has an  intrinsic meaning it's a beautiful thing  that people are coming from way  way more  different varying diverse backgrounds  