hat makes good ones  and in general rule number one is they  shouldn't  require  a cognitive leap some sort of a eureka  moment they should be solvable by  bringing  to bear constructive problem-solving  techniques right you should be able to  explore the problem and ask questions  about the problem and then answer those  questions yourself it shouldn't require  like some clever shift of perspective to  like suddenly see the elegant solution  to the problem because then it then it  just becomes a bit of a coin flip  daniel's rule number two  is that ideally the skills required for  a given problem should be in some way  applicable to the job content itself if  it's not connected in some way then why  are they asking this problem what are  they testing if you do well on this  problem or not how does that correlate  to your likelihood of success on the job  so you know asking really difficult  problems actually i think generally is  counterproductive because realistically  most of us as software engineers don't  solve really difficult problems every  day oftentimes we're working on like  connecting systems together and so  companies need to come up with questions  that apply a lot of requirements they  have to be analogous to the job and  media and novel enough to get a good  signal and are fair game to ask based on  that role and this is the other place  where interviews can break it's tough to  come up with a question that works in a  variety of situations and again it takes  time and energy from engineers to do  this of course daniel says that once you  can find a good solution you get a lot  of mileage out of it i've been using the  same handful of coding questions for  many years now i would say there's an  advantage to using the same question  hundreds of times as an individual is  that you become highly calibrated right  you've seen people make every mistake  possible on this problem uh you've seen  people approach it in many different  ways you're aware of what the pitfalls  are right so you can you you end up  getting really good at  at giving hints so once companies figure  out how to evaluate the candidate and  give them a good problem to solve then  these technical interviews start to  become less broken than what they get  credit for and even then the interview  is not going to be perfect because you  can only get so many signals from data  structures and algorithms type questions  like you might miss out on how someone  might work on problems collaboratively  if they can work independently and ship  code if they can communicate technical  topics fluently to a lot of different  people and if they can take feedback on  their code gracefully and so of course  there's other types of technical  interviews to get signals for stuff like  this programming interviews take home  projects live coding interviews  technical behavioral questions because  here's the thing there isn't a one  interview question to solve it all there  isn't a one-size-fits-all interview type  that will unbreak interviews entirely  and that's because every team every code  base and every company is unique i would  really hesitate from  saying there's like a universally good  interview format in which if everyone  did this the problem is solved it's also  an evolving thing right just like any  product development process let's try  something now that we've tried something  the entire industry as a whole is  realizing there are cons to the ways  that we're doing this right and so as an  entire industry we're all honestly  trying to figure out better way better  ways to do that so i don't think there's  a silver bullet and i also think that  the right choice will differ from  company to company how and what code  gets built has real world implications  like what business needs does that  company have or what technical  challenges does that code base present  or what engineers are on the team and  what kind of values do they have when it  comes to writing clean code it would be  disingenuous to create like a  standardized test for whether a software  engineer can really code because  everyone is looking for something  different of course every team and  company has the responsibility to build  out a thoughtful interview process that  works for them and arguably some of them  can really work on their interview  processes but i don't think technical  interviews are entirely broken i think  maybe they're just not perfect and i'm  not sure if they'll ever be perfect  across the industry because it all  depends on the individuals teams and  companies to do what feels right for  them i feel like there's a lot of like  anger towards  the interview process i think it's kind  of counterproductive for people to get  to  there's like places that are going to be  design interview processes that aren't  aligned with your skills and that's not  a good fit for you and that's great you  figure that out by not passing that  process and there's gonna there's a  company that's meant for you  and their interview hopefully is  designed in a way that like aligns with  your skills and so yeah keep an open  mind type of thing yeah thank you all so  much for watching today's video and  thank you so much to sophie daniel and  adam for being a part of it if you'd  like to support my channel to allow me  to continue making these videos in the  future and you'd like some technical  interview advice because i know that  it's hard when you're going through it  then make sure to become a member where  i'll share the advice that daniel and  adam had for anyone going through the  interview right now make sure to  subscribe to my channel to see more  videos like this and i'll see you next  time bye


